## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to translate a security incident into a comprehensive, end-to-end remediation plan that spans logging hygiene, secrets management, memory safety, access controls, and incident response.

## Key Concepts

- Secrets lifecycle management with short lease expirations
- Logging redaction and tamper-evident logging
- In-memory secret handling and secure memory scrubbing
- Per-tenant isolation and least-privilege access controls
- Incident response playbooks, rollback procedures, and post-mortem hardening

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: redact secrets in logs
function redactSecrets(obj) {
  const SECRET_PATTERNS = [/^(api[_-]?key|secret|token|password)$/i, /vault/i];

  return JSON.stringify(obj, (key, value) => {
    if (SECRET_PATTERNS.some(p => p.test(key))) {
      return '[REDACTED]';
    }
    return value;
  });
}

// Example: secure memory scrubbing
function clearSecretBuffer(buffer) {
  if (buffer && buffer.fill) {
    buffer.fill(0);
  }
}

// Example: tamper-evident log entry
function createLogEntry(secretKey, event, previousHash) {
  const timestamp = Date.now();
  const payload = `${timestamp}|${event}|${secretKey}|${previousHash}`;
  const hash = crypto.createHmac('sha256', process.env.LOG_SECRET)
    .update(payload)
    .digest('hex');

  return { timestamp, event, hash, previousHash };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical monitoring and defense against credential stuffing in a multi-tenant context, focusing on observable signals, mitigations, and operator visibility.

## Key Concepts

- Per-IP and per-tenant rate limiting
- Sliding window counters
- MFA enforcement for risk
- Centralized logging and alerts
- Synthetic traffic validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for login rate limiter using Redis
const { RateLimiterRedis } = require('rate-limiter-flexible');
const redisClient = require('redis').createClient();

const limiter = new RateLimiterRedis({
  points: 5, // attempts
  duration: 60, // per 60s
  storeClient: redisClient
});

// In login route
async function login(req, res) {
  try {
    await limiter.consume(req.ip);
    // authentication logic
    res.send('OK');
  } catch {
    res.status(429).send('Too many attempts');
  }
}
```

